Because this blueprint's `[requires]` pins `context_tokens = 8192` and
`tool_calling = false`, `ack init` will only recommend models that fit a
genuinely small, local footprint.

## Running the demo

```sh
make demo   # python -m app.main — fully offline, no --offline flag needed: there is no online mode
make check  # compile-check + test the generated project
```

At the end of every run you'll see one of two panels:

```
✓ 0 bytes egressed — all inference stayed on this device
```

or, if something regressed,

```
✗ 143 bytes egressed to public-cloud — this run did NOT stay fully on-device
```

The second panel is not hidden or softened — an honest failure here is
the entire point of this blueprint.

## Scope — read this before you extend it

This blueprint produces **decision support**: a plain-language summary of
an intake record, for documentation and patient communication. It does
**not** diagnose and does **not** recommend or select treatment.

All bundled sample data is synthetic. Do not point this demo at real PHI
without first confirming your `ack.toml` policy configuration matches
your organization's requirements — this blueprint defaults to
`egress = "device"` precisely so that decision is deliberate, not
accidental.

## Ejectable: this is your code now

Nothing in this project imports "the framework" and calls back into it.
`app/` is plain Python that happens to import `agenticcarekit.kernel` the
same way it would import any other dependency. Delete `agenticcarekit`
from `pyproject.toml`, inline the bits you use, and it still runs.
Generated once, owned forever.
